.. raw:: html

    <div class="sphx-glr-thumbcontainer" tooltip="Debye and Hückel (1923) showed that each ion&#x27;s cloud of counter-ions lowers its activity below its concentration, by an amount growing with ionic strength and ionic charge. This example uses nernst_potential_with_activity (built on chemistrykit.solutions.systems.activity) to show how the potential of a Cu2+/Cu electrode drifts away from the ideal Nernst value as the solution gets more concentrated, and that the correction vanishes in the dilute limit.">

.. only:: html

  .. image:: /api/gallery/electrochem/nernst/images/thumb/sphx_glr_plot_02_debye_huckel_activity_thumb.png
    :alt:

  :doc:`/api/gallery/electrochem/nernst/plot_02_debye_huckel_activity`

.. raw:: html

      <div class="sphx-glr-thumbnail-title">Debye-Hückel activity corrections to the Nernst equation</div>
    </div>


.. only:: not html

 * :doc:`/api/gallery/electrochem/nernst/plot_02_debye_huckel_activity`

.. raw:: html

    <div class="sphx-glr-thumbcontainer" tooltip="Daniell&#x27;s 1836 two-fluid cell separated a zinc electrode in zinc (or acid) solution from a copper electrode in copper sulfate. Copper metal, not hydrogen gas, is deposited at the cathode, so the cell does not polarize. This example reproduces its standard potential with standard_cell_potential, balances the overall reaction with balance_redox_reaction, and uses the Nernst equation to show how gently the voltage falls as the cell discharges -- the steadiness that made it the first practical voltage standard.">

.. only:: html

  .. image:: /api/gallery/electrochem/standard_potentials/images/thumb/sphx_glr_plot_01_daniell_cell_thumb.png
    :alt:

  :doc:`/api/gallery/electrochem/standard_potentials/plot_01_daniell_cell`

.. raw:: html

      <div class="sphx-glr-thumbnail-title">The Daniell cell: a steady 1.10 V from zinc and copper</div>
